 Optional `closeout_reserve_seconds` stops new work requests that many seconds before the frozen executor `job_timeout`; it must be smaller than `job_timeout`. Choose it from observed request latency and the task's stopping policy, before dispatch. Historical contracts without this field retain their original admission deadline. The deadline starts when the executor launches, after local setup and preflight. The executor receives the limit and its remaining work allowance, together with verified Python/cwd guidance. A completion window is not a guarantee that the model finishes. `job_timeout` still limits the executor process. On process exit or client disconnect, the relay blocks new calls and allows existing responses up to five seconds to finish accounting, then interrupts remaining reservations with unknown usage. Usage is parsed before forwarding response bytes; an unavailable final receipt is never replaced with zero. Errors preserve the local timeout alongside any unknown-budget failure. This cleanup cannot reconstruct usage lost in a historical run.
 
-When available, a bounded `x-request-id` response header is saved as `upstream_request_id` for later reconciliation; it is distinct from Telos's internal request ID. Raw provider response bodies and credentials are not logged. An interrupted CLI-issued thread identity is retained for an authorized same-thread continuation, while the task remains unaccepted and all budget/turn gates still apply.
+### Opt-in automatic work evaluation
+
+`telos_send` accepts optional `evaluate_on_completion` (boolean, default `false`). When `true`, one successful completed work turn for an ownerless, standalone, workflow-v3 implementation session may start exactly one normal `telos_evaluate` job through the existing capacity, lease and budget admission path. The flag is not accepted for owned, project, audit, legacy or non-work contexts. It is not inherited by later sends or repairs.
+
+The work delivery remains durable if automatic admission is refused. `telos_status` exposes `auto_evaluation` with `requested`, `starting`, `started`, `blocked` or `skipped`, source/evaluation job IDs, and a fixed bounded reason code. Budget blockers set `next_action=inspect_budget_before_retry`; inspect usage instead of blindly evaluating or retrying. Interrupted or refused one-shot attempts are inspected manually and never auto-retried. An automatically started evaluation still requires the unchanged checks, same-executor closed-book exam and original supervisor grading; it is not acceptance.
+
+When available, a bounded `x-request-id` response header is saved as `upstream_request_id` for later reconciliation; it is distinct from Telos's internal request ID. Raw provider response bodies and credentials are not logged.
+
+Failed requests may also include optional `failure_stage` (`relay`, `upstream_open`, `upstream_read`, `response_validation`) and `failure_type` (`timeout`, `tls`, `connection`, `os_error`, `value_error`, `other`) metadata. Both fields are persisted only together on a failed request; invalid, partial or successful-completion metadata is omitted. A legacy or successful record without them means the failure stage/type is unknown. These values are derived from local relay operations and exception classes, never from provider messages, payloads or class names. They are local diagnostic evidence, not proof of an upstream root cause, and do not grant retry permission or change request status, usage, latency, budgets, admission, timeout or model traffic. An interrupted CLI-issued thread identity is retained for an authorized same-thread continuation, while the task remains unaccepted and all budget/turn gates still apply.
 
 `dependency_paths` explicitly grants read-only access to required local dependency directories during work/checks. It does not permit writes or networking and is not granted during exams. `command_network=true` permits command networking during work; each private check has its own `network` field. Install dependencies through an appropriate workflow before launching isolated work; Telos does not automatically install a project's dependencies.
 
